SERVICES

A wide range of services exist for the Jabberwocky Toolkit.

TECHNICAL DATA

Dialogue Bus

The Dialogue Bus is the key component of the Jabberwocky Toolkit. The
Dialogue Bus is a nonprivileged, user level program that provides a
mechanism for integrating applications that use standard input (keyboard)
and standard output (screen) channels (i.e., terminal based applications).
The Dialogue Bus can be used to integrate applications that are running on
any platform to which a terminal emulation session can be established (e.g.
IBM, ICL, Bull, Hewlett Packard, VAX, Alpha AXP, PDP-11, and so on). Because
of its asynchronous multitasking capability, the Dialogue Bus can manage
'conversations' with several legacy application simultaneously.

Script Processor (Script)

The Script Processor is a simple programming language that has been
specifically designed for the Dialogue Bus environment.

Motif Menu Manager (MenuMan)

Allows new Motif user interfaces to be generated from simple ASCII text
definition files. This allows the development of a common user interface,
optimized for a particular business process, but often accessing several non
Motif applications.

DECterm Terminal Manager

Enhances the capability of the standard DECterm to allow dynamic,
application specific pull down menu bars and application control of
placement, size, color, iconized and de-iconized, and visible and concealed
attributes.

Motif 'Hot' Image Manager (Himage)

Allows 'Hot Spots' to be interactively associated with X11 bitmaps or GIF
image files. These 'hot' images can then be used as a user interface to
terminal based applications.

Screen Monitor Program (SMP)

SMP is an advanced, rule based, event driven ANSI compliant "screen
scraper." Connecting an application to SMP via the Dialogue Bus allows
applications to respond to screen based events such as appearance of error
text, text attribute, or color change. SMP can also be used to "scrape" data
off an applications structured screen output.

Transition Network Tool (TNT)

TNT is used to create and manage a matrix of events, actions, and data
expressions. A spreadsheet model changing a data item will cause TNT to
evaluate all other "cells" that are affected by the change. This in turn may
cause messages to be dispatched to other applications on the Dialogue Bus.
Combined with SMP, TNT allows complex interactions between legacy
applications to be easily and reliably monitored and controlled. TNT can
also be used as an information store between applications and services
connected to the Dialogue Bus.

StarStream-DB

Layered on the DECMessageQueue persistent messaging protocol, StarStream-DB
allows Dialogue Bus environments to communicate with each other over a wide
area network.

StarStream for Windows (SPD 54.62)

Like StarStream-DB, StarStream for Windows is layered on DECMessageQueue.
Implemented as a Visual Basic Custom Control, StarStream for Windows enables
application programmers to develop Windows applications that communicate
with application services provided by Dialogue Bus environments. StarStream
for Windows also supports the standard (PASCAL) Windows API calling
convention so that it can be used from most programming environments
including C and C++ bindings.

StarStream for Windows also supports Dynamic Data Exchange (DDE) so that,
using the Jabberwocky Toolkit, a 20 year old IBM 3270 based application can
now be used as a data server to an Excel spreadsheet and accessed using a
Windows user interface.

StarStream for Windows Software Developer's Kit

The Software Developer's Kit contains a development version of the runtime
custom control and language bindings file that allows application
programmers to make use of the facilities offered by StarStream for Windows.
The package also includes the Programming and Configuration Guide and
debugging tools that will aid both programmers and system managers in
establishing a StarStream for Windows environment.

